course: biology
lesson: 304
title: Ants of the world: weaver ants, bullet ants, honeypot ants and trap-jaw ants
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A 2022 study estimated there are about 20 quadrillion ants on Earth (a 2 with 16 zeros after it), about two and a half million for every person, and more than 14,000 known kinds. Each kind has its own trick.
explain: Weaver ants sew leaves into nests, holding their own larvae like glue sticks that spin silk. Honeypot ants turn some workers into living jars of sweet liquid. Trap-jaw ants snap their jaws so fast they can fling themselves into the air.
explain: Watch a line of ants crossing the kitchen floor. There is no leader in front; each ant follows a scent trail laid by others, and if you wipe your finger across it, the ants in that spot are lost for a while.
explain: Ants are strong for their size because weight shrinks faster than strength as an animal gets smaller. That is why a tiny ant can carry a crumb many times its weight, and why a giant ant in an old film would collapse.
fact: Weaver ants of Asia, Africa and Australia build nests from living leaves: workers pull the leaf edges together while others hold larvae that spin silk to glue them.
fact: Writings from China about 1,700 years ago describe farmers putting weaver ant nests in citrus trees to eat pests, one of the oldest known uses of one animal to control pests; farmers in Australia and Africa still use them on mango and cashew trees.
fact: The bullet ant of Central and South America has a sting said to be one of the most painful of any insect, feeling like being shot and lasting many hours; Justin Schmidt put it at the top of his pain scale.
fact: Honeypot ants have workers called repletes whose bellies stretch full of sweet liquid; they hang from the nest ceiling as living food stores, and Aboriginal people in Australia have long dug them up as a sweet treat.
fact: Trap-jaw ants lock their jaws open and snap them shut in one of the fastest movements in the animal world, catching quick prey like springtails, and they can snap against the ground to fling themselves away from danger.
fact: An ant's narrow waist has one or two small bumps called nodes; it lets the ant bend its rear end any way to sting, bite or spray, and counting the nodes helps scientists tell ant kinds apart.
fact: A 2022 study estimated about 20 quadrillion ants on Earth, a 2 with 16 zeros after it; all of them together still weigh less than all the people on Earth.
fact: Ants follow scent trails laid by other ants; a line of ants is made by feet and smell, not by a leader.
fact: Scientists put tiny stilts on Saharan desert ants and they walked too far past their nest, while ants with shortened legs stopped short; the ants measure distance by something like counting steps and use the sun for direction.
fact: Matabele ants of Africa raid termites, and when an ant is hurt the others carry it home and lick its wounds, which helps it survive, scientists reported from 2017.
fact: Small ants can carry many times their own weight, and weaver ants were photographed holding about 100 times their weight upside down.
fact: Like all insects, ants have six legs, three body parts and two feelers.
exercise: What do weaver ants use as glue to stick leaves together? | Silk spun by their own larvae | Mud mixed with spit from the workers | Sticky sap that drips from the tree | Wax made by the workers in their bellies
exercise: What did farmers in China do with weaver ants about 1,700 years ago? | Put their nests in citrus trees to eat pests | Ate them as their main food all through the winter | Used them to sew clothes | Kept them as pets in glass jars by the fire
exercise: Where do farmers still use weaver ants to protect trees? | On mango and cashew trees in Australia and Africa | On apple trees in the orchards of snowy northern Canada | On pine trees in Antarctica | Nowhere any more
exercise: What is the bullet ant famous for? | One of the most painful stings of any insect | Being the very smallest ant anywhere in the world | Making the sweetest honey of any ant | Flying faster than a bullet
exercise: Where does the bullet ant live? | In the rainforests of Central and South America | In the middle of the Sahara desert in North Africa | In ordinary British gardens and parks | In the Arctic tundra, under the snow
exercise: What are honeypot ant repletes? | Workers whose bellies are stretched full of sweet liquid | Queens that lay honey eggs instead of ordinary white ones | Ants that steal honey from bees | Little jars the ants build out of wax and mud
exercise: What is special about the jaws of a trap-jaw ant? | They snap shut in one of the fastest movements in the animal world | They are made of honey | They can only open, never close | They are as long as the ant's body and never move, even when it eats its food
exercise: How does a trap-jaw ant escape danger? | It snaps its jaws against the ground and flings itself away | It plays dead for a whole day until all the danger has gone | It changes colour to match the soil | It digs down faster than a mole
exercise: What does an ant's narrow waist let it do? | Bend its rear end any way to sting, bite or spray | Breathe in and out through the middle of its body | Store food for the winter | Hear sounds from far away
exercise: About how many ants are there on Earth, according to a 2022 study? | About 20 quadrillion | About 20 thousand ants | About 20 million ants | About 20 billion ants
exercise: What leads a line of ants across the floor? | A scent trail laid by other ants | The queen walking proudly at the front | A leader ant shouting orders to the rest | The light shining in from the window
exercise: What happened when desert ants were given tiny stilts? | They walked too far and missed their nest | They walked exactly home, just as they did before | They refused to walk at all | They gave up and flew home instead
exercise: What do Matabele ants do for an injured nestmate? | Carry it home and lick its wounds | Leave it behind at once for the termites | Eat it on the spot | Bury it deep in the sand by the nest
exercise: Why is an ant strong for its size? | Weight shrinks faster than strength as size gets smaller | Ants eat special strong food | Ants have tiny bits of steel inside their legs | Ants lift with their feelers as well as their legs and jaws
exercise: How much weight were weaver ants photographed holding upside down? | About 100 times their own weight | Exactly their own weight, no more | Only about half their own body weight | About a million times their weight
exercise: How do scientists tell some ant kinds apart? | By counting the bumps, called nodes, on the waist | By listening carefully to the ants sing in their nests | By weighing the queen | By the colour of the ants' eggs
test: Count them: 5 ants walk across your plate. How many legs is that? | 30 | 20 | 40 | 10
test: Count them: in A Bug's Life the ants have two arms and two legs. How many legs does a real ant have? | 6 | 4 | 2 | 8
test: Count them: a nest has 3 weaver ants pulling a leaf and 4 more holding larvae. How many ants is that, and how many feelers? | 7 ants and 14 feelers | 7 ants and 7 feelers | 12 ants and 24 feelers | 7 ants and 28 feelers
test: «bro there's one ant boss at the front telling the rest where to go» Is that right? | No, each ant follows a scent trail, there is no leader in front | Yes, the queen always walks at the front and leads every line | Yes, the biggest ant always leads | No, the ants follow the sun only
test: In an old film a giant ant the size of a bus stomps across a city. What would really happen? | It would collapse, because weight grows faster than strength as size grows | It would be even stronger for its size, and could lift the buildings | It would fly away easily | It would carry the whole city
test: You wipe your finger across a line of ants. Why do the ants in that spot stop and wander? | You wiped away the scent trail | You scared the leader ant away for good | Your finger is too hot for them to cross | They stop to count their steps again
test: «got stung by a massive ant in the rainforest, it's nothin innit» What is best? | It could be a bullet ant, with an extremely painful sting; tell a guide or adult | Ant stings never hurt | Rub it with another ant to cure it, that's what the locals do | Go and poke the nest to check
test: What would happen if a desert ant's legs were shortened? | It would stop short of its nest | It would walk exactly home anyway | It would walk too far past its nest | It would forget where north is at once
test: Why are honeypot repletes useful when there is no food outside? | They feed the other ants from their stored liquid | They go out and find new food far away | They lay extra eggs | They dig deeper tunnels to reach water
test: A Matabele ant loses a leg in a termite raid. What would its nestmates most likely do? | Carry it home and lick its wounds | Leave it behind for the termites to eat | Give it to a spider | Make it the new queen of the nest
test: Why might a farmer prefer weaver ants to sprays on some trees? | The ants eat pests and leave no chemicals on the fruit | Sprays are always illegal | Ants make the fruit much sweeter by dripping their honey on it | Ants water the trees
exam: Identify the means by which weaver ants join the edges of leaves in the construction of their nests. | Silk produced by larvae held by worker ants | Wax secreted from glands upon the abdomen | Mud transported from the ground below | Resin collected from the bark of trees
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the experiment in which Saharan desert ants were fitted with stilts. | The ants overshot their nest, indicating that they measure distance by a form of step counting | The ants returned precisely to their nest, indicating that they rely solely upon smell | The ants were unable to walk and remained stationary | The ants abandoned their nest and constructed another
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the repletes of honeypot ants? | Workers whose abdomens are distended with liquid food, serving as stores for the colony | Queens that produce honey in place of eggs and are fed by the workers in return | Soldiers that defend the nest with stings | Larvae that spin silk for the nest
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which small animals such as ants may carry loads many times their own weight. | Weight diminishes more rapidly than strength as body size decreases | Ants possess metallic reinforcement within the joints of their legs | Ants consume exceptionally nourishing food | The loads carried by ants are always hollow
